    "answer": {
      "short": "Bob wrongly assumes that $x=-1$ remains excluded. The actual domain is $\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{0,1\\}$; $x=-1$ is allowed.\n\nThe input to $f$ is $g(x)$, so require $g(x)\\ne-1$, while retaining $x\\ne1$ from $g$.",
      "worked": "The input to $f$ is $g(x)$, so the outer restriction is $g(x)\\ne-1$, not $x\\ne-1$. The inner function also requires $x\\ne1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{x-1}+1}\\\\f(g(x))&=\\frac{1}{\\frac{x}{x-1}}\\\\f(g(x))&=\\frac{x-1}{x}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe simplified denominator requires $x\\ne0$. Retaining the original restriction $x\\ne1$, the domain is $(-\\infty,0)\\cup(0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$.\n\nAt $x=-1$, $g(-1)=-\\frac12$ and $f(g(-1))=\\frac{1}{-\\frac12+1}=2$. Thus $x=-1$ is allowed: Bob applied the restriction on the input of $f$ directly to $x$ instead of to $g(x)$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
